--- a client that dials the server in the same VM
a GET with a Content-Length body came back whole: true true
and the length it declared is the length it sent: true
the reason phrase survived the round trip: true
a POST with a body was echoed: true true
the path and query reached the router intact: true
params= sends one parameter and not two: true
params= extends a query the URL already has: true
a repeated key goes out repeated: true
a chunked response was decoded: true true
and it never had a Content-Length to go by: true true
a HEAD came back with headers and no body: true true
and it still declared the length the GET would have sent: true
a 204 has no body and no framing header: true true true
a 404 is an answer, not an exception: true true
a caller's own header went out: true
a one-megabyte body arrived in one piece: true
--- a body delimited by the connection closing
no Content-Length and no chunks, and the body still arrived: true true
and there was nothing in the response that said how long it was: true true
--- a head that arrives one packet at a time
a head split across five reads parsed as one head: true true true
--- and the failures a caller has to be able to tell apart
a peer that never answers hits the deadline: true
a peer that hangs up without answering says so: true
a body shorter than its Content-Length is not quietly truncated: true
a body larger than max_body is refused before it is held: true
nothing is listening: true
https is refused before a socket exists: true
--- stream() hands back a live connection
a body was copied out chunk by chunk, never held twice: true true
and close() is safe to call again: true
streaming works on a close-delimited body too: true
--- every call was its own connection, and every one of them closed
the server accepted one connection per fetch: true true
and none of them was still open at the shutdown: true true
